ou are given the head of a singly linked list. Your task is to return the number of nodes in the linked list.

Example 1:
Input: head = [1, 2, 3, 4, 5]
Output: 5

Example 2:
Input: head = [8, 6]
Output: 2

------------------------------------------

A Node has:
a data part (stores value)
a next part (stores address/reference of the next node)

class Node {
    int data;
    Node next;
    }
we declare a class Node because we use Node word while calling for next nodes and every node contains a data which is a integer and the address of next node

  Node(int data) {
        this.data = data;
        this.next = null;
    }
When we create a new node like:
Node head = new Node(10);
We must tell the program:
What value to store → this.data = data;
The next node is not connected yet → this.next = null;
this refers to the current object.
this.data → the variable inside the Node class
data → the value passed while creating the object



class ListNode{
    int data;
    ListNode next;

    ListNode(int data){
        this.data = data;
        this.next = null;
    }
}
class Solution {
    public int getLength(ListNode head) {
//“This function will start working from the head node, and from there it can move through the whole linked list.”
//Every linked list starts from the head node, and that is what we need to access the full list.
        // Your code goes here
        int count = 0;
        ListNode current = head;
        while(current != null){
            count++;
            current = current.next;
        }
        return count;
    }
}
